2. Advanced Display Filters

Display filters select which packets appear in the packet list. They do not remove packets from the original capture.

2.1 Host and IP filters

Display traffic involving a specific host:

ip.addr == 192.168.1.10

Show packets where a host is the source:

ip.src == 192.168.1.10

Show packets where a host is the destination:

ip.dst == 192.168.1.10

Show traffic between two hosts:

ip.addr == 192.168.1.10 && ip.addr == 192.168.1.20

Exclude a host:

!ip.addr == 192.168.1.10

Filter IPv6 traffic:

ipv6

2.2 TCP filters

Show TCP packets:

tcp

Show TCP traffic on port 443:

tcp.port == 443

Show TCP SYN packets:

tcp.flags.syn == 1

Show initial SYN packets without ACK:

tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0

Show TCP reset packets:

tcp.flags.reset == 1

Show retransmissions:

tcp.analysis.retransmission

Show fast retransmissions:

tcp.analysis.fast_retransmission

Show duplicate acknowledgments:

tcp.analysis.duplicate_ack

Show zero-window announcements:

tcp.analysis.zero_window

Show TCP packets with analysis flags:

tcp.analysis.flags

2.3 UDP and ICMP filters

Show UDP packets:

udp

Show UDP traffic on port 53:

udp.port == 53

Show ICMP packets:

icmp

Show ICMP echo requests:

icmp.type == 8

Show ICMP echo replies:

icmp.type == 0

2.4 DNS filters

Show all DNS packets:

dns

Show DNS queries:

dns.flags.response == 0

Show DNS responses:

dns.flags.response == 1

Show DNS errors:

dns.flags.rcode != 0

Show queries for a particular domain:
dns.qry.name == "example.com"

Show DNS traffic using TCP:

tcp.port == 53

DNS can also use encrypted transports, such as DNS over HTTPS, which ordinary DNS filters will not necessarily decode as DNS messages.

2.5 HTTP filters

Show HTTP traffic:

http

Show HTTP requests:

http.request

Show POST requests:

http.request.method == "POST"

Show GET requests:

http.request.method == "GET"

Show HTTP responses:

http.response

Show HTTP error responses:

http.response.code >= 400

Show a specific HTTP host:

http.host == "example.com"

Show a specific URI:

http.request.uri contains "/login"

These HTTP fields generally apply to traffic Wireshark can dissect as HTTP. HTTPS application content is encrypted unless suitable decryption is configured.

2.6 TLS filters

Show TLS traffic:

tls

Show TLS ClientHello messages:

tls.handshake.type == 1

Show TLS ServerHello messages:

tls.handshake.type == 2

Show TLS certificate messages when present:

tls.handshake.type == 11

Show TLS alerts:

tls.alert_message

Show TLS records on TCP port 443:

tcp.port == 443 && tls

TLS 1.3 encrypts much of the handshake after ServerHello. Certificate visibility and other details depend on the protocol version, capture point, and whether decryption is available.

2.7 Frame and size filters

Show frames larger than 1,400 bytes:

frame.len > 1400

Show frames smaller than 100 bytes:

frame.len < 100

Show packets containing a particular byte sequence in packet data:

frame contains "password"

Use byte-string searches carefully. A match does not prove that a credential was transmitted; it may be unrelated text or data.

2.8 Combining filters

Show DNS queries from one host:

ip.src == 192.168.1.10 && dns.flags.response == 0

Show HTTP requests and DNS traffic:

http.request || dns

Show TCP retransmissions involving a host:

ip.addr == 192.168.1.10 && tcp.analysis.retransmission

Show HTTP errors excluding a specific host:

http.response.code >= 400 && !ip.addr == 192.168.1.20

Tip: Use "&&" for AND, "||" for OR, and "!" for NOT. Parentheses can make complicated expressions easier to understand.

