9. Exporting Objects and Data

9.1 Export HTTP objects

Use:

File → Export Objects → HTTP

Export only the objects needed for the investigation and keep them in a controlled directory.

9.2 Export selected packets

1. Apply a display filter.
2. Select the desired packets.
3. Open File → Export Specified Packets.
4. Choose the required packet range and output format.

Review the dialog options carefully; saving selected packets is different from exporting reassembled application objects.

9.3 Save filtered packets using TShark

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -Y "dns" -w dns-only.pcapng
Save only packets matching a TCP retransmission filter:

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -Y "tcp.analysis.retransmission" -w retransmissions.pcapng

These commands create new capture files containing the packets that match the display filter.

9.4 Extract fields into a report

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -T fields \
  -e frame.number \
  -e frame.time \
  -e ip.src \
  -e ip.dst \
  -e _ws.col.Protocol

The output is tab-separated by default and can be redirected into a text file:

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -T fields \
  -e frame.number -e ip.src -e ip.dst -e _ws.col.Protocol \
  > packet-summary.tsv

