10. Capture Filters vs. Display Filters

These two filter types serve different purposes.

Capture filters

Capture filters limit which packets are collected. They use the capture-filter syntax supported by libpcap.

Examples:

Capture traffic involving a host:

host 192.168.1.10

Capture traffic on TCP port 443:

tcp port 443

Capture DNS traffic:

port 53

Capture traffic between two hosts:

host 192.168.1.10 and host 192.168.1.20

Capture TCP traffic but exclude port 22:

tcp and not port 22

Display filters

Display filters operate on packets already captured and use Wireshark's own filter syntax.

Examples:

ip.addr == 192.168.1.10

tcp.port == 443

dns

tcp.analysis.retransmission

Key difference: Capture filters can permanently omit packets from a capture. Display filters merely hide nonmatching packets from the current view.

