11. TShark: Advanced Command-Line Analysis

TShark is Wireshark's command-line analyzer. It is useful for repeatable investigations, automation, and processing large capture files.

11.1 Read a capture

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng

Show only DNS packets:

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -Y "dns"

Show TCP retransmissions:

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -Y "tcp.analysis.retransmission"

11.2 Extract DNS fields

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -Y "dns.flags.response == 0" \
  -T fields \
  -e frame.time \
  -e ip.src \
  -e dns.qry.name \
  -e dns.qry.type

11.3 Extract HTTP request details

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -Y "http.request" \
  -T fields \
  -e frame.time \
  -e ip.src \
  -e ip.dst \
  -e http.request.method \
  -e http.host \
  -e http.request.uri

Field availability depends on the protocols decoded in the capture.

11.4 List TCP conversations

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -q -z conv,tcp

List UDP conversations:

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -q -z conv,udp

Display protocol hierarchy statistics:

tshark -r network-capture.pcapng -q -z io,phs

11.5 Inspect available fields

Search for fields related to TCP:

tshark -G fields | grep -i tcp

Search for DNS fields:

tshark -G fields | grep -i dns

Search for TLS fields:

tshark -G fields | grep -i tls

This is useful when a filter fails because a field name differs from what you expected.

