12. Investigating Suspicious Traffic

Wireshark can help investigate unusual patterns, but no single filter proves that an attack has occurred.

12.1 Possible port-scanning activity

Look for repeated initial SYN packets:

tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0

Then examine the source address, destination ports, time range, and whether connections completed.

A high number of SYN packets may indicate scanning, but normal applications can also initiate many connections.

12.2 Repeated TCP failures

tcp.flags.reset == 1 || tcp.analysis.retransmission

Investigate the associated conversations and determine whether the behavior is caused by network problems, service configuration, or suspicious activity.

12.3 Unusual DNS activity

dns.flags.response == 0

Investigate repeated queries, unexpected domains, and unusual query patterns. Compare results against the host's expected behavior.

12.4 Cleartext HTTP

http

Inspect whether sensitive information is being sent without encryption. Do not copy real credentials or tokens into reports.

12.5 Unusual outbound connections

tcp.flags.syn == 1 && tcp.flags.ack == 0
Combine this with endpoint and conversation statistics to investigate new outbound connections. You will need environmental context to decide whether a destination is unusual.

12.6 Large packet bursts

frame.len > 1400

Use I/O Graphs and conversation statistics to investigate traffic volume. Large frames alone do not demonstrate data exfiltration or an attack.

