13. Advanced Wireshark Settings

13.1 Enable name resolution carefully

Open:

View → Name Resolution

Depending on the version, you can enable or disable network-name resolution and other name-resolution options.

Name resolution can make captures easier to read, but it may generate DNS lookups and can obscure the original numeric addresses. For forensic work, preserve the raw IP addresses and document any resolution settings.

13.2 Configure coloring rules

Open:

View → Coloring Rules

Coloring rules help visually distinguish packet types and conditions. Built-in rules often highlight retransmissions, resets, and other TCP events.

For a custom rule:

1. Open Coloring Rules.
2. Add a new rule.
3. Enter a descriptive name.
4. Set the display filter.
5. Choose foreground and background colors.
6. Save the rule.

Example filter for TCP resets:

tcp.flags.reset == 1

13.3 Customize columns

To add useful fields to the Packet List:

1. Select a packet.
2. Expand the desired field in Packet Details.
3. Right-click the field.
4. Choose Apply as Column, if available.

Useful columns include:

- Source and destination IP
- Source and destination port
- TCP stream index
- TCP flags
- TCP sequence number
- DNS query name
- HTTP request method
- HTTP host
- HTTP response code

Custom columns make large captures easier to investigate.

13.4 Configure TCP analysis preferences

Open:

Edit → Preferences → Protocols → TCP

Review options related to TCP analysis, reassembly, and sequence-number handling. Available settings depend on your Wireshark version.

Do not disable reassembly merely to make a capture look simpler: it may prevent higher-level protocols from being decoded correctly.

13.5 Set the time display format

Open:

View → Time Display Format

Choose a format suitable for your investigation, such as time of day or seconds since the beginning of the capture.

Relative timing is useful when analyzing delays; absolute timestamps are useful when correlating packet events with other logs. Verify the capture's timestamp accuracy and time zone before drawing conclusions.

13.6 Configure protocol dissection

If Wireshark decodes traffic as the wrong protocol, inspect the packet's port and protocol details. The Decode As option can help in authorized analysis where a service uses a nonstandard port.

Use this carefully: forcing a protocol decoder does not prove that the traffic actually follows that protocol.

13.7 Manage capture options

Before starting a capture:

1. Choose the correct interface.
2. Set an appropriate capture filter if needed.
3. Choose a manageable file size and capture duration.
4. Consider ring-buffer options for long-running captures.
5. Avoid capturing unrelated traffic.
6. Confirm where the capture file will be stored.

For live investigations, rotating files can help prevent a capture from consuming all available disk space.

