15. Practical Investigation Workflow

Use this sequence in a lab or approved investigation.

Step 1: Identify interfaces

ip -br addr
tshark -D

Step 2: Capture the relevant traffic

sudo tshark -i eth0 -w lab-capture.pcapng

Stop the capture when enough evidence has been collected.

Step 3: Inspect the protocol hierarchy

tshark -r lab-capture.pcapng -q -z io,phs

Step 4: Identify the relevant conversations

tshark -r lab-capture.pcapng -q -z conv,tcp

Step 5: Investigate specific protocols

DNS:

dns

HTTP:

http

TCP retransmissions:

tcp.analysis.retransmission

Step 6: Follow the relevant stream

Select a packet, right-click, and use Follow → TCP Stream or the appropriate protocol stream option.

Step 7: Export and document evidence

Save a filtered capture or export selected packets if needed. Record:

- Investigation objective
- Capture source and time range
- Tool and version
- Filters used
- Relevant packet numbers and stream IDs
- Actual observed results
- Interpretation and alternative explanations
- Recommended next steps

Documentation rule: Distinguish actual observations from example commands and hypothetical output. A filter returning packets is not, by itself, proof of malicious activity.

